  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ on the interval $[1,2].$",
  "worked_solution": "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}})-(4\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{4}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =\\frac{19}{4} \\\\ 4+\\frac{2}{{c}^{3}} & =\\frac{19}{4} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{c}^{3}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{c}^{3}}{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}^{3} & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{3}} \\\\ c & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
